  "name": "MEAN CORPUSCULAR HAEMOGLOBIN (MCH)",
  "code": "1565",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>It measures the <strong data-start=\"1129\" data-end=\"1161\">average amount of hemoglobin</strong> in a single red blood cell, measured in picograms (pg).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>MCH Result</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low (&lt;27 pg)</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates <strong>hypochromia</strong> (less hemoglobin per RBC). Common in iron deficiency and thalassemia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normal (27&ndash;32 pg)</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates <strong>normochromia</strong> &ndash; normal hemoglobin per RBC.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High (&gt;32 pg)</strong></td>\n<td>May be seen in <strong>macrocytic anemias</strong> &ndash; large RBCs tend to have more hemoglobin. But can also be due to falsely elevated readings in lipemic or high-glucose samples.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
